// XFAIL: RT-12: a format's `.*` precision is read and dropped; `w$` and `1$` widths are accepted and ignored (open)
// A width or precision from an argument (`{:>w$}`, `{:.p$}`, `{:>1$}`,
// `{:>width$}` with `width = 5`) is Rust's; guide formatting says it "is
// not read yet", and guide rust that what is not vole is an error. vole
// type-checks the argument (an f64 there is "a format width or precision
// is an integer") and then ignores it: every line below prints the bare
// value (`a[1]`, `d[3.14159]`, ...). The EXPECT lines are rustc's.
// EXPECT: a[      1]
// EXPECT: c[      1]
// EXPECT: d[3.14]
// EXPECT: e[3.14]
// EXPECT: f[      1]
// EXPECT: g[   3.14]
// EXPECT: h[    1]
fn main() {
    let w = 7;
    let p = 2;
    let x = 1;
    let f = 3.14159;
    println("a[{:>w$}]", x);
    println("c[{x:>w$}]");
    println("d[{:.p$}]", f);
    println("e[{f:.p$}]");
    println("f[{:>1$}]", x, w);
    println("g[{:>w$.p$}]", f);
    println("h[{:>width$}]", x, width = 5);
}
